1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,800
So you spent eleven years at the agency. What finally made you walk?

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:10,600
Honestly? A spreadsheet. Someone sent me a spreadsheet on a Tuesday afternoon.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:16,400
It had every project I'd shipped, ranked by revenue per hour of my time.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,200
And the three things I was proudest of were at the bottom. Dead last.

5
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:27,600
Right, but that's the business working as intended, isn't it? That's the job.

6
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:33,400
That's exactly what my director said. Word for word, almost.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,200
And he wasn't wrong. That's what bothered me. He was completely right.

8
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:45,000
I just didn't want to be right about that particular thing for another decade.

9
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:50,800
Did you have anything lined up? Or was this a jump-and-hope situation?

10
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:56,600
Nothing. I had about seven months of savings and a very patient partner.

11
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:02,400
Which sounds reckless, and it was, but seven months is longer than it sounds.

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,200
The first two months I did nothing. I mean genuinely nothing. I gardened.

13
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:14,000
People always skip that part when they tell this story. They jump to the pivot.

14
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:19,800
But you cannot think clearly about what's next for at least six weeks.

15
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:25,600
Your brain is still running the old job in the background. It takes that long.

16
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:31,400
So what broke the fog? Was there a moment, or did it just accumulate?

17
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,200
A friend asked me to look at her pricing. Just as a favour, over coffee.

18
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:43,000
She was charging forty pounds an hour for something worth about four hundred.

19
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:48,800
I fixed it in ninety minutes. She tripled her income that quarter.

20
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:54,600
And I thought, well. I've been doing that for free at parties for eleven years.

21
00:01:55,200 --> 00:02:00,400
That's a very tidy origin story. Is it true, or is it the version you tell?

22
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,200
It's the true one, but you're right to ask. I have a tidier one for podcasts.

23
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:12,000
The real version has a lot more crying in it and a much worse first client.

24
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:17,800
Tell me about the worst first client. That sounds considerably more useful.

25
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:23,600
He wanted a strategy deck. Forty slides. He had already written thirty of them.

26
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:29,400
And every single one of them was wrong in a way he was emotionally attached to.

27
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,200
I took the money. That was the mistake. I knew on the first call and I took it.

28
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:41,000
What would you charge for that job now? Assuming you'd take it at all.

29
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:46,800
I wouldn't. And that's the actual answer to your first question, I think.

30
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:52,600
Leaving wasn't about money. It was about being allowed to say no to things.

31
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:58,400
Eleven years of not being allowed to say no, and then one Tuesday spreadsheet.
